2.1.1 Percentage distribution of population size and growth rate by LGA
In the context of development, the mere size of a population does not mean much unless it is
linked to available resources. The relation ship between population growth and economic
development cannot be over -emphasized as both are  inextricably linked . Additional people
can be beneficial in creating more wealth with the potential to advance development. Yet i n
most African countries, rapid population growth has led to slow economic growth with
implications on development.
Rapid population growth has a strong impact on a country’s ability to provide  basic social
services, such  as education and health care. T he effect of rapid population growth on the
environment is quite evident.  In many regions of the world, particularly the Sahel, the soil is
being washed away, forest  covers are disappearing, deserts are spreading and wildlife being
threatened, and water r esources depleting. The rapid growth of population translates into
increased human needs and intensified pressure on available resources. As a result, the
quantity and quality of natural resource base tends to decline.
The total population of The Gambia  on April 15 th 2013 was 1,857,181 persons ( 913,755
males and 943,426 females). This is an increase of 49 6,500 persons over the 2003 figures of
1,360,681. The population growth rate declined  from 4.2 per cent per annum in 1993 to 2.7
per cent in 2003 and has increased slightly to 3.1 per cent per annum in 2013 (refer to annex,
Table B.2). Annex Table B.1 shows that the population of The Gambia has increased steadily
over the past 3 decades from about half a million in 1973, to about 1.9 million in 2013.
The Brikama LGA has the largest population followed  by the Kanifing with 37.1 and 20.3
per cent respectively of the total population . The Banjul, Mansakonko, Kuntaur and
Janjanbureh LGAs had the lowest proportions with 1.7, 4.4, 5.2 and 6.7 per cent respectively
of the total population. The Brikama LGA had the highest population growth rate (5. 7 per
cent) followed by Basse (2.6 per cent) whilst Banjul had a negative population growth rate  (-
1.2 per cent ).  The negative population growth rate for Banju l could be attributed to out -
migration of the population mainly to the Kanifing and Brikama LGAs. In fact, Banjul LGA
experienced its first negative population growth rates of (-0.4 per cent ) and (-1.9 per cent)
respectively f or the 1983 -1993 and 1993-2003 inter-c

