2.1.4 Distribution of the population by religion
According to the 2013 Census, the population of The Gambia is predominantly Muslim (96 .0
per cent) followed by Christianity (3.8 per cent). Traditionalist and people who practice other
religions each accounted for 0.1 per cent.
2.2 Population density
In 2013, the overall population density was estimated at 17 3.9 persons per square kilometre ,
one of the highest in the S ub-Saharan Africa. Over the years, the population density of The
Gambia has increased from 64 persons per square kilometre in 19 83 to 97 persons per square
kilometre in 19 93 and further increased from 127 persons per square kilometre in 2003 to
173.9 persons per square kilometre in 2013.
Banjul, Kanifing and Brikama LGAs had the highest population densities . In 2013, the
population density of Kanifing (4, 991.8 persons per square kilometre) surpasse d that of
Banjul (2 ,539.2 persons per square kilometre) , while  the Brikama LGA had a population
density of 390.4 persons per square kilometre. Unlike Kanifing, where the population density
continues to rise,  Banjul's population density has been declining  in the past three decades.
This could be attributed to the fact tha t the city is  an island and there is no more room for
human settlement , thus, there has been migration of people from  Banjul to Kanifing  and
Brikama (refer to annex, Table B.3).
2.3 Population by age and sex
The demographic significance of age and sex cannot be over -emphasized. These variables
determine the future demographic trends and are in turn influenced by the dynamics of
population (i.e. fertility, mortality and migration).
During the census information on  age and sex of persons were collected.  The person’s age
was recorded in complete years as at 15 th April 2013. For persons who did not know their
ages, a calendar of events was used to assist them estimate their ages. This was meant to
reduce the incidence of people who reported they don’t kn ow their ages and also to improve
on the coverage and accuracy of age reporting.
According to the 2013 Census, The Gambia has an overall median age of 18.2 years with
17.8 and 18.5 years respectively for males and fem ales. The median  age divides the
population into two halves i.e. the age at which 50 .0 per cent of the population are above and
50.0 per cent are below. These low median ages mean that the country has a relatively young
population.

