4.3 Female Gambian teenage population (12-19 years) by marital status
and ethnicity
Table D.1  (refer to annex, Table D.1) shows the percentage distribution of the female
Gambian teenage population (12–19 years)  by marital status. The data shows that early
marriage within this age group has reduced. Out of a total Gambian teenage population of
176,727 which represents 9.5 per  cent of the total population enumerated in T he Gambia in
2013, 91.7 per cent reported to beNever married       and only 6.6 per cent were married. The
proportion of the teenage population divorced, separated and widowed were all less than one
percent each. Among the ethnic groups, Fulas (9.2 per cent) and Sarahules (9.4 per cent) have
the highest proportion of the teenage populatio n that are married. They also have the lowest
proportions of the teenage population that areNever married      .
4.4 Gambian population aged (12-19 years) by educational attainment
and marital status
Table 4.4 shows the percentage distribution of the teenage population by marital status and
educational attainment.  It is observed from the table that the proportion of the teenage
population married is higher among those with  no education (54.1 per cent)  followed by
those with primary education (19.9 per cent) and the least proportion was those with tertiary
education (0.3 per cent) . It is also shown that a higher proportion of teenage Gambian
population who were either divorced (37.3 per cent)  or separated (40.4 per cent)  have no
education. Overall, a higher proportion of the teenage population  had attained either Lower
secondary or primary education (30.6 per cent and 30.2 per cent respectively).
